why 2 1/4 TENDENCY The tenor leaps up from G to D, the root of the dominant, instead of falling to F♯. The alto already takes F♯, so the tenor fills in the root.
why 2 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION A cadence where every resolution falls: soprano A–G, the alto's F♯ drops to D, tenor D–B♭ and the bass D–G.
why 2 3/4 DOUBLING The tune leaps up to D for the new phrase while the alto keeps D from the fermata chord. The doubled third keeps the held note.
why 5 1/4 SPACING The bass walks down a scale, D–C–B♭–A–G–F♯, into its low register while the tenor stays high. The wide gap gives this cadence a deep, full sound.
why 5 3/8 SPACING The bass continues its descent to A; the tenor holds G above.
why 5 1/2 SPACING The bass reaches a low G; the tenor drops to E♭, but the gap is still wide.
why 5 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION All voices step down together toward the cadence while the bass continues its descending scale.
why 5 5/8 SPACING The bass touches F♯, its lowest point, before resolving to G.
why 5 3/4 PROGRESSION The chord over the bass's F♯ is a diminished seventh, F♯–A–C–E♭, which works as a dominant. So the move to i is a normal resolution.
why 8 1/4 SEVENTH The seventh B♭ of the C⁷ chord does resolve to A, but in the soprano, which falls B♭–A into the fermata. The tenor outlines G–C instead.
why 8 1/4 LEAPS After its diminished leap down to E the bass falls further to C and then to F for the cadence. Bach treats E as part of the C⁷ chord, so the bass moves through chord notes rather than turning back.
why 10 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION Every voice falls into iv⁶ as the last phrase heads for the cadence; the tenor drops an octave, the others move by step.
why 10 5/8 LEAPS The alto walks down C–B♭–A–G–F♯ by step. A scale line hides the tritone between its ends.
why 10 3/4 DOUBLING A passing ⁶₄ over the bass's descent E♭–D–C♯ to the dominant. Its doubled B♭ follows the soprano and tenor lines.
why 11 0 LEAPS The bass falls G–E♭–D–C♯ toward the dominant. Its ends form a tritone, but it moves mostly by step, and C♯ resolves to D at once.
why 11 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION The piece ends on G major, a Picardy third. Every voice falls into it: the seventh C to B, the alto's F♯ to D and the bass to G.
